.\" ezmk-lua(1) - standalone Lua hook runtime (1.4.3)
.TH EZMK\-LUA 1 "2026-09-19" "EazyMake 1.4.3" "User Commands"
.nh
.SH NAME
ezmk\-lua \- standalone Lua hook runtime for EazyMake
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ezmk\-lua
.I hook.lua
.RB [ \-\-project\-root
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-\-profile
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-\-output
.IR path ]
.br
.B ezmk\-lua
.B \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ezmk\-lua
executes a single Lua hook script with the
.B ezmk.*
API registered. It is the companion runtime used by
.B ezmk project export cmake
to re\-run the
.B pre_build
and
.B post_build
hooks of
.BR [hooks] :
the generated CMake calls
.B ezmk\-lua
instead of linking the whole build tool, so hook post\-processing keeps working
under a plain CMake build.
.PP
The binary ships next to
.B ezmk
in every distribution channel. It runs one script and exits; it never loads a
project configuration for a full build and it is
.B not
the runtime behind
.B ezmk utils
or package install hooks (those use the sandboxed in\-process runtime).
The generated CMake looks it up with
.BR "find_program(EZMK_LUA ezmk\-lua)" ;
when the program cannot be found the hook is skipped with a CMake warning, and
.B on_failure
hooks are never exported (CMake has no equivalent).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-project\-root " " \fIdir\fR
Project root used to resolve
.B ezmk.toml
and every relative path exposed by the
.B ezmk.*
API. Defaults to the current directory. When no
.B ezmk.toml
exists there, the configuration readers degrade to empty values instead of
failing.
.TP
.BR \-\-profile " " \fIname\fR
Profile name stored in
.BR ctx.profile .
It selects which profile sections
.RB ( [compile.profile.<name>] ", " [link.profile.<name>] )
the surrounding CMake configuration was generated for.
.TP
.BR \-\-output " " \fIpath\fR
Path of the produced artifact, stored in
.BR ctx.output
\(em typically
.B $<TARGET_FILE:...>
of the exported target.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit with status 0.
.PP
Unknown options, a missing option value and a missing
.I hook.lua
operand are usage errors: usage is printed and the exit status is 2.
.SH HOOK CONTRACT
The script is loaded as an ordinary Lua chunk and must define a global
function
.BR run :
.nf
function run(ctx)
    \-\- ctx.output        (string, may be empty)
    \-\- ctx.project_root  (string)
    \-\- ctx.profile       (string, may be empty)
    return 0
end
.fi
.PP
A chunk that raises an error, or that never defines
.BR run ,
aborts the run: the message is printed and the exit status is 1. The value
returned by
.B run
becomes the process exit status; a non\-numeric return value counts as 0. A
Lua
.B error()
call likewise prints the message and exits with status 1.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The script runs in the
.B full
Lua global environment: the standard libraries are available, including
.B io
and
.BR os .
This is a strict superset of the sandbox used by
.BR "ezmk build" ,
and
.B [utils.permissions]
is
.B not
enforced here \(em the runtime is a developer tool outside the trust boundary
of package code and is therefore never used to execute third\-party scripts.
.PP
Because the two environments differ in what they
.I allow
but not in the
.B ezmk.*
functions they expose, hooks that must behave identically under
.B ezmk build
and under an exported CMake build should restrict themselves to the
.B ezmk.*
API subset documented in
.IR docs/en/utils.md .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The hook returned 0 (or a non\-numeric value).
.TP
.B 1
Lua error, missing
.B run()
function, or a non\-zero value returned by the hook.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error (unknown option, missing option value, missing script operand).
.SH FILES
.TP
.BR scripts/*.lua
Typical location of hook scripts referenced by
.BR [hooks] ;
paths in
.B ezmk.toml
are resolved relative to the project root.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ezmk (1),
.BR ezmk.toml (5),
.BR ezmk\-workspace.toml (5)
.PP
Full specification:
.IR https://github.com/3667808244/EazyMake
(section
.B hooks
of
.IR docs/en/config_file.md ,
and the
.B ezmk
Lua API in
.IR docs/en/utils.md ).
